Arbitrate the attach APN between IMS and data, and ask for P-CSCF

This CP grants the PDN its attach APN names and refuses to create a second
one, answering MATCH_ALL_RULE_NOT_ALLOWED (2253). Measured both ways round:
with the attach on 'ims' the IMS PDN comes up and data is refused 2253 for
*both* carriers' data APNs; with the attach on the carrier APN that APN
connects and 'ims' is the one refused. So the attach APN is a single slot with
two claimants, and whichever one holds it is the one that works.

(An earlier version of this patch claimed the CP refuses the APN *string*
'ims'. That was wrong. The injection test it rested on was run while the
tested APN already was the attach APN, so it resolved to the existing attach
context instead of creating one -- the very mechanism this patch relies on.
Android does run IMS and data together on this CP, so a second PDN is not
impossible; the field worth looking at next is SETUP_DATA_CALL v3's
match_all_rule_allowed at @0x105, which is the one byte named after the
refusal.)

Parity stays the default: every boot attaches the way Android does, on the
carrier's IA entry, and a CP that grants the IMS PDN on request never does
anything else. Only once the CP has actually refused does the modem re-attach
on the carrier's IMS APN, which makes the attach itself create the IMS context
so the SETUP_DATA_CALL that follows only has to resolve to one that exists.
Nothing has to be configured by hand -- no nmcli gsm.initial-eps-bearer-apn.

Data outranks VoLTE in that choice, because taking the attach APN is what stops
a data PDN being created at all:

  - the escalation never fires while a data connection is up or in flight.
    CONNECTING counts, not just CONNECTED: on this modem the two land in the
    same log second, and testing CONNECTED alone lost the race and pulled the
    attach out from under a connect that was about to succeed.
  - MMSitClient gained a "data-pdn-refused" signal, carrying the CP's cause
    from both refusal paths. On 2253 while the IMS attach is in force the modem
    puts it back: it restores the carrier's entry and cycles the radio to
    produce an attach, because sending the APN alone changes nothing -- the CP
    keeps serving the attach it already has. A flag then settles the choice in
    data's favour for the rest of the power-up so the two cannot flip-flop.

On a dual-SIM phone this reaches a usable split on its own: data on the SIM
that carries it, VoLTE on the SIM that does not.

The P-CSCF request follows the same condition. Stock asks for nothing at @0xdb
because its CP grants the IMS PDN on request and the addresses arrive with it;
once the attach is what creates the context, a later SETUP_DATA_CALL returns
the parameters the context was created with, so asking there is too late and
the network grants no addresses at all. Measured: fallback with pcscf 0 leaves
AIMS_REGISTRATION at length 36 forever, and asking in the attach takes it to
159 with a real SIP URI.

Two cid-bookkeeping fixes come with it, both exposed by the above. A SETUP that
is sent but never granted an address creates no context, so there is nothing to
deactivate: the CP answers DEACTIVATE_DATA_CALL with status 2, the cleanup
counts as failed and the cid is retained "until radio-off" while the per-stack
table still maps it to the APN -- so cid_acquire() hands the same one back and
mm_sit_cp_claim_data() refuses it, and every retry for the rest of the process
fails with "context N is already connected or awaiting setup/cleanup". One
refused data call wedged a SIM's data for a whole boot. Only tear down what
actually came up, and give up the stale mapping when the cid is reserved
elsewhere.

Also splits the carrier-config directory in two, which is not cosmetic: the CP
is told /vendor/firmware/carrierconfig, the string the vendor RIL sends, so the
CARRIER_CONFIG_MANIFEST frame stays byte-identical, while the plugin opens its
own cfg.db at the real rootfs path and exynos-modem's RFS server re-roots the
CP's opens.

Verified on a Pixel 9 Pro XL with two SIMs (TELUS, Fizz): across a cold boot
and a ModemManager restart, VoLTE registers with a real SIP URI on the SIM
without data while the other carries data with ping at 0% loss, and the
arbitration runs with no churn -- it defers rather than escalating and walking
it back.

